  "@graph": [
    {
      "@id": "bioschemasdrafts:TrainingMaterial",
      "@type": "rdfs:Class",
      "rdfs:comment": "A specification for describing training materials in life sciences. The Life Science Training Materials specification provides a way to describe bioscience training material on the World Wide Web. It defines a set of metadata and vocabularies, built on top of existing technologies and standards, that can be used to represent events in Web pages and applications. The goal of the specification is to make it easier to discover, exchange and integrate life science training material information across the Internet. Version: 1.2-DRAFT.\n\n<h3>Summary of Changes</h3>\n<br>\nChanges since previous draft 1..1-DRAFT of the TrainingMaterials profile:\n<ul>\n<li>Add assesses, MANY, optional, it comes from LerningResource</li>\n<li>Add educationalAlignment, MANY, optional, it comes from LerningResource</li>\n<li>Add educationalUse, MANY, optional, it comes from LerningResource</li>\n<li>Move abstract from recommended to optional as description is more commonly used</li>\n<li>Add associatedMedia, MANY, optional, use it to point to distributions of this training material in case</li>\n<li>Add conditionsOfAccess, ONE, optional, useful also for other research artifacts and aligned (somehow) to FAIR accessibility</li>\n<li>Add creditText, ONE, optional, aligned to RO-Crate, it would include the text to cite this item</li>\n<li>Add funding, MANY, optional, good practice for any research artifact, connect to funder from here</li>\n<li>Add isAccessibleForFree, ONE, optional, useful information to be used with, for instance, filters as part of findability</li>\n<li>Add publisher, ONE, optional, datePublished already included, if used it makes sense also to have the publisher</li>\n<li>Add sourceOrganization, MANY, optional, useful to directly link to organizations providing this training material, good for recognition</li>\n<li>Add applicationDomain, MANY, recommended, useful for findability and filtering, it should come from a extension to CreativeWork, included here to facilitate discussion in parallel about its use in TrainingMaterial</li>\n<li>Add contactPoint, MANY, optional, useful to know how to contact a first point of entry, it should come from a extension to CreativeWork, included here to facilitate discussion in parallel about its use in TrainingMaterial</li>\n<li>Add sourceRepository, ONE, optional, useful to link to a repository if the material was created there and it is open, it should come from a extension to CreativeWork, included here to facilitate discussion in parallel about its use in TrainingMaterial</li>\n<li>Add nextVersion, ONE, optional, useful if the material is versioned, it should come from a extension to CreativeWork, included here to facilitate discussion in parallel about its use in TrainingMaterial</li>\n<li>Add previousVersion, ONE, optional, useful if the material is versioned, it should come from a extension to CreativeWork, included here to facilitate discussion in parallel about its use in TrainingMaterial</li>\n</ul>\n<br>\nAcknowledgement note: changes in this draft come from information gathered during some hackathons sponsored by NFDI4DS (DFG grant number 460234259), ConnOSS (DFG grant number 561044496), DiscoRSE (Klaus Tschira Foundation, scientific software grant), German BioHackathon 2026 (organized by deNBI).",
      "rdfs:label": "TrainingMaterial",
      "rdfs:subClassOf": {
        "@id": "schema:LearningResource"
      },
